  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Responsiveness of quantity demanded to price. The third option describes elasticity of supply and the fourth is total revenue.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Quantity rises 50% and price falls 20%, so 50 ÷ 20 = 2.5. Using the new price as the base gives 2.0, which is the trap.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Equal percentage changes give an elasticity of one, which is unit elastic and leaves total revenue unchanged.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Below one is inelastic: quantity responds proportionately less than price.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    A vertical curve means the same quantity is demanded whatever the price, so the elasticity is zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    A horizontal curve means any rise in price destroys demand entirely, so the elasticity is infinite.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Quantity rises proportionately more than price falls, so the product of the two rises. This is the revenue test.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Quantity falls proportionately less than price rises, so total revenue rises — which is why taxes are heavy on inelastic goods.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Unit elasticity. Revenue rises through the elastic range, peaks at one, and falls through the inelastic range.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Inelastic demand: the extra output depresses the price by more than the quantity rises, so revenue falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    A single brand has many close substitutes, which is the strongest determinant of elasticity.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Inelastic: few substitutes and a small share of income, so quantity barely responds to price.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Time allows substitution and adjustment, which is why the long-run elasticity exceeds the short-run one.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    High, or elastic: a large share of income makes a given percentage price rise impossible to ignore.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Quantity supplied to price. It is always positive, because the supply curve slopes upward.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    The quantity is fixed, so the supply curve is vertical and the elasticity is zero.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Spare capacity allows output to rise quickly, which is what elasticity of supply measures.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    5 ÷ 10 = 0.5, positive and below one, which is a normal necessity. Above one would be a luxury.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Demand falls as income rises, which is the definition of an inferior good.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Both changes are in the same direction, so the elasticity is positive and the goods are substitutes.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    One good becoming dearer reduces demand for the other, which is what complements do — car and petrol.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    No relationship at all: a change in the price of one leaves demand for the other untouched.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    The sign, not the size, names the relationship: positive means substitutes.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    The less elastic side cannot walk away, so it absorbs more of the tax whatever the formal arrangement.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Inelastic demand: quantity barely falls, so the revenue is raised without much change in behaviour.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    30 ÷ 20 = 1.5, elastic, so the quantity effect dominates and revenue rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Along a straight line the elasticity varies: perfectly elastic where it meets the price axis and perfectly inelastic where it meets the quantity axis.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    The percentage fall in price is exactly matched by the percentage rise in quantity, so the product does not change.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Cars and petrol are complements. The other three pairs are substitutes, whose cross elasticity is positive.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    High, because a buyer can switch when the price rises — substitutes are the strongest determinant of elasticity.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    The harvest is fixed once planted, so the short-run response is small; in the long run farmers can plant more.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Elastic: the quantity response outweighs the price cut, so revenue rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    2 ÷ 5 = 0.4, which is inelastic. Dividing the percentages the other way round gives 2.5, the elastic answer.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Elastic demand means a price cut raises revenue, because the quantity effect is larger than the price effect.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    A negative cross elasticity marks complements: one dearer means less of the other is bought.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Above one: demand rises proportionately more than income, which is what makes a good a luxury.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "D", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Time and capacity. The other options are determinants of demand, not of supply.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    25 ÷ 10 = 2.5, elastic. The 0.4 figure reverses the division, which is the most common arithmetic error in the topic.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Percentage changes against absolute changes. Elasticity is unit-free, which is why it can compare salt and cars.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 12.51pt)[
    Below one is inelastic supply: quantity supplied responds proportionately less than price, which is typical of a good that takes time to produce.
  ]

]

